/* Generated by tools/build.js from data/google-ace.js. Edit that file, not this one. */
CertHub.addPlanDetail("google-ace", {"study":{"1":[["How are IAM allow policies and organization policies inherited in the resource hierarchy?","Both are set on the organization, a folder or a project and apply to everything below. IAM allow policies are additive: a principal's effective access is the union of grants at the resource and every ancestor, so a lower level cannot take away an inherited grant (that needs an IAM deny policy). Organization policies restrict what can be configured and can be inherited, merged or overridden at lower levels, depending on the constraint."],["What is the difference between a project name, project ID and project number?","The name is a changeable display label. The project ID is globally unique, chosen at creation (or generated) and cannot be changed; gcloud and APIs use it. The project number is assigned by Google and is used internally, for example in default service account names."],["Why grant roles to groups rather than individual users?","Groups let you manage access by changing membership in Cloud Identity or Google Workspace instead of editing IAM policies on many resources. Joiners and leavers are handled in one place, and policies stay short and auditable."],["What does a Cloud Billing budget do, and what doesn't it do?","It tracks actual (and optionally forecasted) spend against an amount for a billing account or selected projects and sends alerts at thresholds, by email or to Pub/Sub. It does not cap or stop spending by itself; automatic action requires a Pub/Sub notification handled by your own code."],["What are named gcloud configurations for?","A configuration holds properties such as account, project, compute/region and compute/zone. You create several (for example dev and prod) and switch with `gcloud config configurations activate`, so commands go to the right project without adding flags every time."],["What should you do when a deployment fails because of a quota?","Check the quota's usage and limit on the Quotas page (IAM and admin > Quotas) for the right region and metric, then request an increase, or reduce the request, use another region, or clean up unused resources."]],"2":[["How do you choose between Compute Engine, GKE, Cloud Run and Cloud Run functions?","Compute Engine gives full OS control for lift-and-shift or special software. GKE runs containers with Kubernetes APIs and control over scheduling. Cloud Run runs stateless containers serverlessly, scaling to zero. Cloud Run functions run small event-driven pieces of code. Move right when you want less to manage, left when you need more control."],["Explain managed instance groups and why instance templates matter.","A managed instance group (MIG) creates identical VMs from an instance template and can autoscale, autoheal with a health check, and roll out updates. Templates can't be edited, so to change the machine type or image you create a new template and start a rolling update."],["Compare GKE Autopilot and Standard.","Autopilot manages nodes for you and bills mainly for the resources pods request, with Google applying security and scaling best practices. Standard gives you control over node pools, machine types and node settings, and you pay for the nodes. Regional clusters replicate the control plane across zones for availability."],["Match workloads to Cloud SQL, Spanner, Bigtable, Firestore and BigQuery.","Cloud SQL: regional relational databases (MySQL, PostgreSQL, SQL Server). Spanner: relational with horizontal scale and strong consistency, including multi-region. Bigtable: wide-column NoSQL for huge, low-latency key-based reads and writes such as time series. Firestore: document database for web and mobile apps. BigQuery: serverless data warehouse for SQL analytics."],["Describe the Cloud Storage classes.","Standard for frequently accessed data; Nearline, Coldline and Archive are cheaper to store but charge for retrieval and have minimum storage durations (30, 90 and 365 days). All classes have the same low latency when you do read the data. Lifecycle rules can change class or delete objects by age or other conditions."],["Auto mode vs custom mode VPC?","An auto mode VPC gets one subnet per region automatically from 10.128.0.0/9. Custom mode lets you create only the subnets you want with your own ranges, which avoids overlaps with on-premises or peered networks and is recommended for production."]],"3":[["How can you SSH to a VM that has no external IP?","Use IAP TCP forwarding (`gcloud compute ssh VM --tunnel-through-iap`) with a firewall rule allowing 35.235.240.0/20 on tcp:22 and the IAP-secured Tunnel User role, or connect from a host inside the VPC. OS Login controls who can log in through IAM."],["What's the difference between a snapshot, a custom image and a machine image?","A snapshot is an incremental backup of one disk. A custom image is a boot disk template for creating many VMs (grouped by image family). A machine image captures a whole VM, including configuration and all disks."],["How do HA and read replicas differ in Cloud SQL?","High availability keeps a standby in another zone of the same region and fails over automatically; the standby doesn't serve reads. Read replicas serve read-only traffic to offload the primary and can be in other regions, but they don't fail over automatically in the same way."],["What's the difference between an alerting policy and an uptime check?","An uptime check probes a URL, IP or service from locations around the world. An alerting policy watches a condition (a metric threshold, an uptime check failure or a log match) and notifies channels such as email or Pub/Sub when it is met."],["Which audit logs are always on and which must you enable?","Admin Activity and System Event logs are always written and can't be turned off. Data Access logs (reads of configuration or user data) are off by default for most services, except BigQuery, and must be enabled. Policy Denied logs record access denied by security policies such as VPC Service Controls."],["What can a log sink do?","A sink routes matching log entries to a destination: a log bucket, BigQuery dataset, Cloud Storage bucket, Pub/Sub topic or another project. Aggregated sinks at the organization or folder level can include child projects."]],"4":[["Compare basic, predefined and custom roles.","Basic roles (Owner, Editor, Viewer) are very broad and apply across almost all services. Predefined roles are curated by Google for one service and job. Custom roles contain exactly the permissions you choose, for least privilege when no predefined role fits; they are created at the organization or project level."],["What role do you need to attach a service account to a VM, and why?","Service Account User (roles/iam.serviceAccountUser) on that service account, as well as permission to create the VM. Without it, anyone who can create VMs could run code as any service account and gain its access."],["Why avoid service account keys, and what are the alternatives?","Keys are long-lived secrets that can leak. Prefer attached service accounts for Google Cloud workloads, impersonation (Service Account Token Creator) for people, Workload Identity Federation for workloads outside Google Cloud, and Workload Identity Federation for GKE for pods."],["Compare Google default encryption, CMEK and CSEK.","All data is encrypted at rest by default with Google-managed keys. With customer-managed encryption keys (CMEK) you create and control keys in Cloud KMS (rotation, disabling, IAM) while Google uses them. With customer-supplied keys (CSEK) you provide the raw key with each request and Google does not store it; lose it and you lose the data."],["When would you use a signed URL?","To give someone without a Google identity temporary access to one object, for example a download link valid for an hour. The URL is signed by a service account's credentials and expires, so the bucket can stay private."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Setting up a cloud solution environment","w":23,"topics":["Resource hierarchy: organization, folders and projects, and how IAM and organization policies are inherited","Creating projects: project name, project ID and project number, and enabling APIs","Organization Policy Service: constraints such as resource locations and disabling service account key creation","Managing users and groups with Cloud Identity or Google Workspace, and Google Cloud Directory Sync","Granting IAM roles to users and groups at the organization, folder and project levels","Cloud Billing accounts: linking projects to billing and the billing IAM roles","Budgets, budget alerts and exporting billing data to BigQuery","Installing and configuring the Google Cloud CLI: gcloud init, named configurations, default project, region and zone","Cloud Shell, the Google Cloud console and Cloud Shell Editor","Quotas: viewing usage, understanding quota errors and requesting increases","Regions, zones and labels: placing resources and organizing them for cost reporting"],"notes":["Exam guide section 1: Setting up cloud projects and accounts; Managing billing configuration"],"labs":["Free trial or free tier: create a project from Cloud Shell with `gcloud projects create`, look up its project number with `gcloud projects describe`, enable the Cloud Run API with `gcloud services enable run.googleapis.com`, then list enabled services. Projects and APIs cost nothing on their own; shut the project down when finished.","Free tier: create a budget of $1 on your billing account with alerts at 50%, 90% and 100%, then export billing data to a new BigQuery dataset and look at the table after a day. Budgets and small BigQuery queries stay within the free tier, but remember a budget alerts only and does not stop spending.","Free (no resources): install the gcloud CLI locally or use Cloud Shell, create two named configurations (`gcloud config configurations create dev` and `prod`) with different default projects and regions, switch between them with `activate`, and open the Quotas page to see your Compute Engine CPU quota in one region."]},{"id":2,"name":"Planning and implementing a cloud solution","w":30,"topics":["Choosing a compute option: Compute Engine, GKE, Cloud Run, Cloud Run functions and App Engine","Compute Engine instances: machine families, custom machine types, images and Spot VMs","Compute Engine disks: Persistent Disk and Hyperdisk types, regional disks and local SSD","Instance templates and managed instance groups: autoscaling, autohealing and rolling updates","Google Kubernetes Engine clusters: Autopilot vs Standard, zonal vs regional, and node pools","Deploying workloads to GKE with kubectl: Deployments, Services and Ingress","Cloud Run services and Cloud Run functions: deploying containers and event-driven code with Eventarc and Pub/Sub","Choosing a data product: Cloud SQL, AlloyDB, Spanner, Firestore, Bigtable and BigQuery","Cloud Storage buckets: storage classes, locations, lifecycle rules and Object Versioning","VPC networks: auto vs custom mode, subnets, firewall rules, Shared VPC and VPC Network Peering","Load balancing, Cloud NAT and hybrid connectivity with Cloud VPN and Cloud Interconnect","Infrastructure as code with Terraform and Cloud Marketplace solutions"],"notes":["Exam guide section 2: Planning and implementing compute resources, data storage solutions and networking resources; Deploying infrastructure as code"],"labs":["Free tier: create one e2-micro VM in a free-tier US region (us-west1, us-central1 or us-east1) with a standard persistent disk, SSH to it from the console, install a web server and add a firewall rule allowing tcp:80 only to VMs with the tag `web`. Delete the VM and the rule when done; other machine types, regions and disk types are billed.","Free tier: deploy the sample container `us-docker.pkg.dev/cloudrun/container/hello` to Cloud Run with `gcloud run deploy`, open the URL, deploy a second revision and split traffic 90/10. Cloud Run scales to zero and has a monthly free allowance; delete the service afterward.","Free tier: create a regional Cloud Storage bucket in a US region, upload a few files, turn on Object Versioning, overwrite a file and list noncurrent versions, then add a lifecycle rule that deletes objects after 30 days. Stay within the free storage allowance and delete the bucket when finished."]},{"id":3,"name":"Ensuring successful operation of a cloud solution","w":27,"topics":["Managing Compute Engine VMs: start, stop, resize, SSH with OS Login and IAP TCP forwarding","Snapshots, snapshot schedules, custom images and image families","Managing GKE: scaling Deployments, node pool autoscaling, rollouts and rollbacks, and cluster upgrades","Managing Cloud Run: revisions, traffic splitting, minimum and maximum instances","Managing Cloud Storage objects with gcloud storage and moving data with Storage Transfer Service","Operating databases: Cloud SQL backups, high availability and read replicas, and BigQuery dry runs","Operating networks: expanding subnets, static IP addresses and Cloud DNS","Cloud Monitoring: metrics, dashboards, alerting policies, uptime checks and the Ops Agent","Cloud Logging: Logs Explorer, log-based metrics, log buckets and sinks","Cloud Audit Logs: Admin Activity, Data Access, System Event and Policy Denied logs","Troubleshooting with Error Reporting, Cloud Trace and Cloud Profiler"],"notes":["Exam guide section 3: Managing Compute Engine, GKE and Cloud Run resources; Managing storage and database solutions; Managing networking resources; Monitoring and logging"],"labs":["Free tier: on your e2-micro VM, install the Ops Agent, then in Cloud Monitoring build a dashboard with CPU and memory, and create an alerting policy that emails you when CPU is above 80% for 5 minutes. Run a CPU-heavy command to trigger it. Delete the VM afterward.","Free tier: in Logs Explorer, filter for `protoPayload.methodName` on your project to find who created or deleted resources (Admin Activity audit logs), create a counter log-based metric for a text string, and create a sink that routes those entries to a Cloud Storage bucket. Remove the sink and bucket when done.","Free tier: take a snapshot of your VM's boot disk, create a snapshot schedule that keeps 3 days of daily snapshots, and restore a new disk from the snapshot. Snapshot storage is billed beyond small amounts, so delete the schedule, snapshots and extra disk afterward."]},{"id":4,"name":"Configuring access and security","w":20,"topics":["IAM roles: basic, predefined and custom roles, and least privilege","Viewing and changing IAM allow policies with gcloud, and IAM Conditions","Service accounts: creating them, granting roles, attaching them to resources, and default service accounts","Service account impersonation, short-lived credentials and avoiding service account keys","Workload Identity Federation for GKE and for workloads outside Google Cloud","Identity-Aware Proxy and OS Login for secure administrative access","Encryption: Google default encryption, Cloud KMS customer-managed keys and customer-supplied keys","Secret Manager for passwords, API keys and certificates","Cloud Storage access: uniform bucket-level access, public access prevention and signed URLs","Policy Troubleshooter, IAM recommendations and audit roles for reviewing access"],"notes":["Exam guide section 4: Managing Identity and Access Management (IAM); Managing service accounts"],"labs":["Free: create a service account with `gcloud iam service-accounts create`, grant it Storage Object Viewer on one bucket, grant yourself Service Account Token Creator on it, then run `gcloud storage ls gs://BUCKET --impersonate-service-account=SA_EMAIL`. No key is created, and none of this is billed.","Free: create a custom role that only has `compute.instances.get`, `list`, `start` and `stop`, grant it to a test user or group, and use Policy Troubleshooter to confirm the user cannot delete a VM. IAM itself is free; delete any VMs you started.","Free tier: store a value in Secret Manager, grant a Cloud Run service's service account Secret Manager Secret Accessor, and expose the secret as an environment variable. Then enable uniform bucket-level access on a test bucket and generate a signed URL that expires in 10 minutes. Delete the secret versions, service and bucket afterward; Secret Manager and Cloud Run have small free allowances."]}]});
